```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the BIND9 DNS service on dns_server (10.200.0.2).
The zone file /etc/bind/db.local defines the "local" domain but contains A
records only for the four web servers (web0–web3). It has no A records for
any host computers — specifically host_1_1_1_1 (10.1.1.2) or host_2_1_1_1
(10.2.1.2). Compounding this, named.conf sets "recursion no", so the server
will not forward queries for names outside its authoritative zones. Both
hosts' /etc/resolv.conf points exclusively to 10.200.0.2 with no search
domain configured and no fallback resolver.

When a user on host_1_1_1_1 tries to reach any resource in building 2 by
hostname, the OS sends a DNS query for that name. The server returns REFUSED
(bare name, recursion disabled) or NXDOMAIN (name.local, no record in zone).
Because /etc/hosts only maps localhost and the host's own name, there is no
fallback. Every hostname-based connection attempt to the other building fails
at name resolution — making the entire building appear to have "vanished"
even though the underlying IP fabric is completely healthy (OSPF fully
converged, all routes present, bidirectional ICMP verified).

Root cause: The DNS zone file on dns_server is missing A records for the
host computers in buildings 1 and 2, and recursive resolution is disabled
with no fallback, so no hostname outside the incomplete zone resolves.

Fix: Add A records to /etc/bind/db.local for host_1_1_1_1.local → 10.1.1.2
and host_2_1_1_1.local → 10.2.1.2 (and any other internal hosts), add
"search local" to both hosts' /etc/resolv.conf, then reload BIND
("rndc reload"). Alternatively enable "recursion yes" if an upstream
forwarder is available, but the authoritative records are still needed for
internal names.
=== END ===
```